-- Numeral literals: `3` is `Suc (Suc (Suc Zero))`, the same term.  A
-- literal is a numeral of the type its context gives it; one its
-- context leaves open is the program's one numeral type, here `nat`
-- (SPEC.md section 9b).  A literal is unary: `n` is 8n + 1 atoms.  `toNum` is the case of
-- words-to-numbers, written with literals, and compiles to its term.
nat === Zero | Suc nat
word === One | Two | Three
bool === PTrue | PFalse

toNum w = w |> { One 1; Two 2; Three 3 }
three := toNum Three
answer := natAdd (toNum Two) 40
isSix n = natIfEq n 6 PTrue PFalse
pred n = n |> { Zero 0; Suc m m }
five := pred 6
